# Line-ending policy.
#
# The problem this solves: TASKS.md, README.md, docs/taxonomy.md and six of
# src/synth/*.py were committed from Windows with CRLF, while everything
# written on Linux is LF. Editing a CRLF file from Linux rewrites every line,
# so a five-line change arrives as a 181-line diff, blame is destroyed, and
# two people editing the same file conflict on every line rather than on the
# lines they actually touched. This happened three times in one week.
#
# `text=auto` makes Git normalise text files to LF *in the repository* on
# commit, whatever the author's platform. Working copies still check out in
# each platform's native ending, so nothing changes for the Windows members
# day to day; the repository simply stops storing two different conventions
# for the same kind of file.
* text=auto

# These must be LF in the WORKING TREE, not just in the repository. A shell
# script whose shebang ends in CRLF fails on Ada with
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
# which is an obscure way to lose a job, so it is worth forcing rather than
# leaving to core.eol.
*.sh       text eol=lf
*.sbatch   text eol=lf
*.py       text eol=lf
*.yaml     text eol=lf
*.yml      text eol=lf

# PowerShell is Windows-side tooling and stays CRLF.
*.ps1      text eol=crlf

# Binary: never transform, never diff as text. Most are gitignored, but a
# stray commit of one should not be mangled.
*.png      binary
*.jpg      binary
*.jpeg     binary
*.pdf      binary
*.zip      binary
*.npz      binary
*.npy      binary
*.pt       binary
*.pth      binary
*.safetensors binary
